export const ARTICLE_MARKDOWN = "# Autoflower vs Photoperiod: How Cannabis Decides When to Flower\n\nLast checked: October 27, 2026\n\nMost plants on a windowsill don't care what time it is. Cannabis does. The classic cannabis plant waits for the days to get shorter before it starts building buds. Then there's the other kind, which flowers on its own schedule, no calendar required. That split is the whole autoflower vs photoperiod question, and it's one of the most searched growing terms out there. After Dark Cannabis, at 1664 Jane Street, breaks it down for adults 19+. Not a grow tutorial. A plain explainer, so the words make sense when you hear them.\n\n## Photoperiod cannabis: the light-sensitive original\n\nCannabis is a short-day plant. Translation: it stays leafy and growing (the vegetative stage) while the days are long, and it switches into flowering once the daily dark period gets long enough.\n\nOutdoors, that switch lines up with the shrinking days at the end of summer and into fall. Indoors, growers control it by changing how many hours the lights stay on. A widely used indoor pattern, as described in a 2024 paper in *The Plant Journal*, runs 18 hours of light and 6 hours of dark for leafy growth, then switches to 12 and 12 to trigger flowering. The same paper notes that most drug-type cannabis will stay vegetative under long days if nothing else pushes it to flower.\n\nThat's photoperiod cannabis: the plant reads day length and responds.\n\n## Autoflower cannabis: flowering by age\n\nAutoflowering cannabis ignores the day-length signal. It's called \"day-neutral\" or \"photoperiod-insensitive\" in the research, and it starts flowering once it reaches a certain stage of development, no matter how long the light runs.\n\nWhere did that trait come from? Researchers have long linked it to cannabis populations from high northern latitudes, sometimes classified as *Cannabis ruderalis*. A 2022 study in *Frontiers in Plant Science* describes the trait as proposed to have come from those high-latitude populations, and a 2024 study in *The Plant Journal* identified a second genetic location tied to it, concluding that at least two independent loci are involved. In plain words: there's more than one genetic route to \"auto.\"\n\n## Autoflower vs photoperiod at a glance\n\n| | Photoperiod | Autoflower |\n|---|---|---|\n| Flowering trigger | Shorter days / longer dark period | Plant age |\n| Indoor light schedule | Changed to start flowering | Doesn't need a change |\n| Research term | Short-day | Day-neutral / photoperiod-insensitive |\n| Ancestry talk | Classic cultivated cannabis | Linked to high-latitude populations |\n| On a label | Not stated | Not stated |\n\nRead that last row twice. Whether a product came from an autoflower or a photoperiod plant is not one of the standard lines on a Canadian cannabis label.\n\n## What autoflower does NOT mean\n\nNot a hype word. Some quick corrections:\n\n- **Not a strain.** It's a flowering trait that breeders cross into many different cultivars.\n- **Not a THC signal.** The only reliable cannabinoid numbers are Total THC and Total CBD on a legal label.\n- **Not \"worse\" or \"better.\"** It's a different timing system. That's it.\n- **Not a separate legal category.** Dried cannabis is dried cannabis on the shelf.\n\n## Why growers care about the difference\n\nProducers pick one or the other for practical reasons, and the research spells some out:\n\n- **Timing.** Autoflowers skip the short-day requirement, so nobody has to change the lights to start flowering.\n- **Northern seasons.** The 2024 *Plant Journal* paper notes that auto-flowering makes outdoor growing at high latitudes possible.\n\nYou don't need any of that to buy flower. It just explains why the word keeps showing up.\n\n## Hearing the terms at a counter\n\nHere's how the vocabulary tends to land in a conversation:\n\n- **\"Photo\"**: short for photoperiod.\n- **\"Auto\"**: short for autoflower.\n- **\"Veg\"**: the leafy growth stage before flowering.\n- **\"Flip\"**: switching indoor lights to the flowering schedule. Only applies to photos.\n- **\"Day-neutral\"**: the science name for auto.\n\nNot a quiz. Just useful to know which is which.\n\n## Questions people ask on Jane Street\n\n**Can you tell an autoflower bud by looking at it?** No. Once it's dried and trimmed, the flower doesn't announce how it decided to bloom.\n\n**Is ruderalis a separate species?** Researchers still debate how to classify it. The safe answer: it's a name used for high-latitude cannabis populations linked to the autoflower trait.\n\n**Does the label say which one it is?** Not as a standard line. Check the product class, Total THC, Total CBD, net weight and packaging date instead.\n\n## The bottom line\n\nAutoflower vs photoperiod comes down to one question: what tells the plant to start flowering? Day length, or age. Photoperiod plants wait for shorter days; autoflowers bloom on their own clock. Neither tells you more than the label does.\n\nComing by? The [After Dark visit page](/visit) has the trip details, and the [Jane and Lawrence dispensary page](/jane-and-lawrence-dispensary) covers the area. After Dark Cannabis is for adults 19 and over. Bring government-issued photo ID.";
